"An orchestration DAG shows task prerequisites that determine when work may run in a workflow.\n\nUse when: Review parallelism and prerequisite ordering in a data job. Explain failed, skipped, or blocked task execution.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need exact field lineage rather than execution dependencies.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote tasks or explicitly expanded task groups. Directed edges encode prerequisite dependencies rather than data volume. Run-status markers include a legend and a capture timestamp.\n\nBuild: Select a workflow definition or one observed run. List tasks and verify each dependency condition. Lay out topological ranks, keeping independent branches parallel. Place retries and trigger-rule notes beside tasks without routing edges across node text.\n\nCheck: The dependency graph has no unintended cycles. A downstream task’s trigger rule explains failure and skip behavior. Definition and run-state information are visibly distinguished. Group boundaries and edge labels have generous unobstructed padding.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming all prerequisite edges carry data. Interpreting a green design node as evidence that a production task succeeded.\n\nExample: Extract Orders and Extract Refunds run independently; Reconcile waits for both before Publish executes.\n\nAlternatives: Data lineage diagram, CI/CD pipeline diagram."
